{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 31, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "\"the social network\" is a thought-provoking film that delves into the fascinating story of facebook's creation and the tumultuous journey of its founder, mark zuckerberg. directed by david fincher, the movie is a gripping narrative that explores the complexities of ambition, friendship, and the consequences of success.\n\nthe film's screenplay, written by aaron sorkin, is a masterclass in storytelling. it's a non-linear narrative that jumps back and forth in time, weaving together multiple perspectives to create a rich and nuanced portrait of zuckerberg and his world. the dialogue is witty and incisive, with characters that are multidimensional and relatable.\n\njesse eisenberg's portrayal of zuckerberg is a standout performance. he brings a depth and complexity to the character, capturing both his intelligence and his awkwardness. eisenberg's zuckerberg is a fascinating figure, both repellent and likable at the same time. andrew garfield and justin timberlake also deliver strong performances as eduardo saverin and sean parker, respectively.\n\nthe film's themes are timely and thought-provoking. it explores the consequences of zuckerberg's actions, both personally and professionally, and raises important questions about the nature of success and the costs of ambition. the movie also touches on issues of class, privilege, and the complexities of relationships in the digital age.\n\nfincher's direction is, as always, meticulous and precise. he brings a keen eye for detail to the film, using a muted color palette and a dynamic camera style to create a sense of tension and unease. the score, composed by trent reznor and atticus ross, is equally effective, adding to the film's sense of unease and foreboding.\n\none of the most striking aspects of \"the social network\" is its portrayal of the facebook phenomenon. the film captures the excite